# Copy this file to .env, then adjust the values below if you need to.
#
# DATABASE_URL points at the SQLite file that holds every account, room
# and voyage. The path is relative to prisma/schema.prisma, so
# file:../db/custom.db means db/custom.db in the project root. The file
# is created on first write, so a missing one is normal on a fresh setup.
DATABASE_URL=file:../db/custom.db

# The port the whole game answers on: the site, the API and the realtime
# channel together. Leave it unset to use 8080.
PORT=8080

# The address to bind. 0.0.0.0 lets other devices on your network reach
# the game, which is what a tunnel or a second player on the same wifi
# needs. Use 127.0.0.1 to keep it on this machine only.
HOST=0.0.0.0

# The code that admits an operator account through /admin. The register
# form there accepts no code but this one, so generate your own secret
# rather than copying this example, which is a placeholder and nothing
# more. An unset or empty value refuses every attempt: an installation
# that never sets one has no way in at all.
ADMIN_SETUP_CODE=change-me-to-a-secret-of-your-own

# The share of voyages that leave a telemetry record, as a fraction of
# one: 1 records every voyage, 0 records none, and 0.25 records a
# quarter of them. A record is written once, when a voyage ends, and
# nothing in the game reads one back, so this decides how much the
# harbor remembers rather than how it plays. Leave it unset to record
# every voyage.
TELEMETRY_SAMPLE_RATE=1

# A multiplier on the length of every phase of a round, from 0 to 10.
# 1 is the lengths the game was designed with, so this is for slowing a
# table down or moving it along rather than for tuning: a value above
# one gives a harbor more time in every phase, and a value below one
# gives it less. 0, or the word off, turns the clock off entirely and
# leaves every room advancing the way it did before the clock existed,
# each phase ending only when every captain has readied.
#
# It is off unless it is asked for. A seat that ends because a timer says
# so is a pressure rather than a convenience, and only a mode can decide
# it wants one, so the founding mode keeps no clock whatever this says
# and Gambit keeps none until this is set. Leave it unset for 0.
#
# Nothing about a voyage is stored differently with the clock off, so it
# is a safe thing to set and unsafe in no direction.
PHASE_CLOCK=0

# Everything below this line belongs to Ocean Gambit, the experimental
# voyage this branch is building toward. Each switch is read as two
# questions in a fixed order: whether the room is a Gambit room, and then
# what this file says. So a switch can only ever take a system away from
# the mode it belongs to, and never hand it to the founding mode: a
# Classic harbor plays the game this branch forked from whatever is set
# here, which is the point of the branch rather than a limitation of it.
#
# The provisions layer: the Larder, and the short rations a crew works
# through when it runs empty. Leave it unset to play with it on. 0, or the
# word off, turns the whole layer off: nothing is eaten, nothing is slowed
# and nothing is drawn, and a voyage is stored no differently either way,
# since the Larder is a field every save carries whether or not a rule
# reads it.
#
# The NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix is not decoration and it is the one thing to
# know before relying on this switch. The rules it gates run in the
# browser, where the engine runs, and a value without that prefix is not
# present in a client bundle at all. The cost is that the browser's copy
# of the value is fixed when the bundle is built, so turning the layer off
# takes a rebuild and a restart rather than a restart alone. The server
# reads the same value from the same file when it assembles an audit
# reveal, so both halves move together.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SURVIVAL=1

# The crew's permanence: the loss of a hand after two consecutive legs on
# short rations. Leave it unset to play with it on. 0, or the word off,
# turns the rule off while the named roster stays, so a captain still meets
# named artisans and loses none, and a run of hungry legs in progress
# resets rather than waiting to spring when the rule comes back. The layer
# above governs this one, so a build with NEXT_PUBLIC_SURVIVAL off loses
# nobody whatever this says. The NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix carries the same cost
# and the same rebuild note as the switch above it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CREW_LOSS=1

# The crew's clothes: what they wear against the cold, the warmth those
# garments buy them, and the frostbite one hand takes for a leg spent short
# of it. Leave it unset to play with it on. 0, or the word off, turns the
# rule off while the wardrobe stays a field every save carries, unread
# rather than rewritten: nothing is worn out, nobody freezes, and the
# wardrobe panel is not drawn. The provisions layer above governs this one,
# so a build with NEXT_PUBLIC_SURVIVAL off has no cold whatever this says.
# The NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix carries the same cost and the same rebuild note as
# the switch above it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GARMENTS=1

# The split hold: the two capacities the plan's own rollback names by
# hand, Stores for the larder and Cargo for the trade. It is the riskier
# half of the feature and it ships separately from the foods, which ride
# the provisions layer above rather than this switch. Leave it unset to
# play with it on. 0, or the word off, puts the hold back the way this
# game has always had it: one unbounded pool for goods with nothing
# refused for room, food back under the Larder's own sixty meal ceiling,
# and the panel's two capacity lines not drawn, so the header reads the
# way it read before the split. What it does not reach is the pantry: the
# three foods, their keepings and the preserve above them belong to the
# layer, so a table that rolls the hold back still eats in the same order
# and still watches produce turn. The provisions layer above governs this
# one, so a build with NEXT_PUBLIC_SURVIVAL off plays the base game
# whatever this says. The NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix carries the same cost and
# the same rebuild note as the switches above it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SPLIT_HOLD=1

# The pathbound order board: the three trade cards the paths own, appended
# after the tier's own draw and drawn greyed, each stamped with the crest and
# the sentence of the path that would open it. Leave it unset to play with it
# on. 0, or the word off, puts the board back to the six cards a charter
# schedules and nothing else, which is the board this game has always dealt.
# The switch is read at both ends of a card's life, at the draw and at the
# lock, so a board dealt while it was on plays on as six ordinary orders once
# it is off rather than leaving three cards grey forever, and nothing about a
# voyage is stored differently either way, since a marked card is a marked
# card and this switch is what decides whether the mark means anything. It is
# not one of the provisions switches above and the layer does not govern it,
# because a card on the trade board has nothing to do with what a crew eats.
# The NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix carries the same cost and the same rebuild note as
# the switches above it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PATH_ORDERS=1

# The escort contract: the board a Convoy captain sells a single leg of
# protection from, during Parley, at a price the two captains agree. Leave it
# unset to play with it on. 0, or the word off, refuses every sale, so a
# Convoy captain is refused as flatly as anyone else, no board is drawn and
# the raid roll reads no cover, which is a voyage playing exactly the raid it
# played before this feature existed. A contract already agreed when the
# switch goes off loses its cover rather than being cancelled: the board is
# not drawn from and the engine reads no cover, so the buyer sails on their
# own luck and no claim can be raised. Nothing about a voyage is stored
# differently either way, because a contract is transient room state and a
# save never carries one: what a save carries is the tally of what already
# settled and the ledger of movements already applied, both of which are
# records of the past rather than agreements, and both of which survive the
# switch. Like the order board above it, this is not one of the provisions
# switches and the layer does not govern it. The NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix carries
# the same cost and the same rebuild note as the switches above it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ESCORT_CONTRACTS=1

# The Loom's bench: a garment put right in one leg, sold by a Loom captain
# to another captain at a price the two of them agree, with the harbor
# tailors and the rag pile standing beside it as the alternative every
# other captain has. Leave it unset to play with it on. 0, or the word off,
# closes the bench and leaves the consent primitive it is built on where it
# is: no refit can be offered, no board is drawn, and none of the three
# counters does anything, so no tailor puts a point back, no rag comes off
# the pile and nothing is rewoven. Nothing about a voyage is stored
# differently either way, because a refit is transient room state and a
# save never carries one. The wardrobe layer governs this one and the mode
# governs the wardrobe, so a build with NEXT_PUBLIC_GARMENTS off has no
# bench whatever this says, and a Classic table has none either. The
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix carries the same cost and the same rebuild note as
# the switches above it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_REFITS=1

# The module market: a module bolted to a hull listed at the Parley table
# and bought by another captain at a price the two of them agree, so the
# shipyard ladder stops being a one way street. Leave it unset to play with
# it on. 0, or the word off, closes the market and leaves the consent
# primitive it is built on where it is: no module can be listed, no board
# is drawn, and a trade never settles, so no module leaves the hull it was
# drafted into and the four counters stay still. Nothing about a voyage is
# stored differently either way, because a trade is transient room state
# and a save carries only what a hull holds and what its ledger sold, both
# of which every build reads. This one the mode governs directly: the
# market rides the gambit systems, so a Classic table has none whatever
# this says. The NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix carries the same cost and the same
# rebuild note as the switches above it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_MODULE_TRADES=1

# The bazaar desk: the rumor an Aroma captain may publish about a price,
# and the band shift that rumor leaves behind in the next port. Leave it
# unset to play with it on. 0, or the word off, disables both halves rather
# than half of one: no rumor can be published, the desk is not drawn, and
# the two ends of the settlement read the same switch, so no lean is sent
# on the advance that opens a market and none is folded in when it is
# priced. That is what the plan means by asking that a band shift skipped
# cleanly rather than left half applied, and a rumor already published
# simply stops moving anything. Nothing about a voyage is stored
# differently either way. This is not one of the provisions switches and
# the layer does not govern it: a rumor is a rule about prices, and prices
# are in the base game. The NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix carries the same cost and
# the same rebuild note as the switches above it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_BAZAAR=1

# The path draft: three cards dealt to every captain when the voyage leaves
# the dock, one kept at each of three steps, with the card left at the end
# being the path that captain sails. Leave it unset to play with it on. 0,
# or the word off, deals nothing and leaves every captain pathless, which
# is the voyage this game has always sailed: nothing before this feature
# ever wrote a captain's path, so with the draft off the deal is not made,
# no card reaches anyone, and the pathbound order board falls back on the
# refusal it already answers, since a pathless captain is locked out of
# every pathbound card by the lock rule rather than by a second one. The
# one switch covers the change of papers as well, because a captain can
# only hold a path the deal gave them: with the draft off there is nothing
# to change away from, so the switch has no precondition to meet. The
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix carries the same cost and the same rebuild note as
# the switches above it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PATH_DRAFT=1

# The milestone boons: a boon dealt at each of the five moments a voyage
# can mark, a hand lost, a first pathbound order filled, a rung of the
# merchant ladder crossed, a cold leg weathered, and the fleet's
# commission answered. Leave it unset to play with it on. 0, or the word
# off, arms no moment, so nothing is dealt at any of the five: a moment
# left waiting in a save written while the switch was on is simply not
# shown, and a boon already held keeps its effect for the voyage, because
# nothing this switch does unwrites a card that was given. The load path
# is the unwinding road, the same one every other saved field heals on: a
# held boon the record no longer knows is dropped as it loads. Three of
# the five moments lean on another system's switch as well (the loss rule,
# the wardrobe and the pathbound lock), so a build with one of those off
# answers no moment from it whatever this says. The NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix
# carries the same cost and the same rebuild note as the switches above
# it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_MILESTONE_BOONS=1

# The charter moment: one trio offered at leg four, the captain's own two
# path charters first and a wildcard drawn beside them, one kept, each
# card forking that path into a sub build for the voyage. Leave it unset
# to play with it on. 0, or the word off, offers no trio, so the moment
# passes unshown: a charter left waiting in a save written while the
# switch was on is simply not shown, and a charter already held keeps its
# flags for the voyage, because nothing this switch does unwrites a card
# that was given. The load path is the unwinding road, as with the
# milestone boons above: a held charter the record no longer knows is
# dropped as it loads. A captain with no path is offered nothing whatever
# this says, because a charter forks a path and there is nothing to fork.
# The NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix carries the same cost and the same rebuild note
# as the switches above it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CHARTERS=1
